\begin{abstract}
The Spider cipher is a manual stream cipher operated with a deck of
$n=40$ cards. Its state is the deck order, an element of the symmetric
group $\Sn$, and each step left-multiplies the state by one of $n$
fixed permutations: a cut at a chosen index followed by a fixed
``back-front'' (Monge) shuffle, optionally followed by a single
transposition (the \emph{perfect} variant). The reachable states thus
form the Cayley graph of the group these permutations generate. We
prove this graph is vertex-transitive, prove that the plain generators
generate $\Sn$ for \emph{every even} $n$---so the graph is connected on
all $n!$ states---by closed-form identities uniform in $n$, with the
perfect variant handled at $n=40$, and study its \emph{injectivity
radius} or
\emph{free depth}---the largest $r$ for which all distinct length-$\le
r$ generator words give distinct states. We establish the girth of the
plain variant is exactly $9$ by a meet-in-the-middle search over all
cut sequences, relate the free depth to a counting (birthday) bound
$\tfrac12\log_n n!\approx 15$, and define the \emph{relation leak
rate} $\sum_L c_L/n^{L}$, where $c_L$ counts length-$L$ relators; we
compute $c_9=1,\ c_{10}=0$ for the plain variant and $c_9=c_{10}=0$ for
the perfect variant. Finite assertions (a specific relator, the
meet-in-the-middle search, the relator counts, and an independent
Schreier--Sims order computation) are machine-checked; the structural
results are proved. The constructions use only standard tools from the
theory of random walks on the symmetric group, applied to a generator
set that does not appear to have been studied before.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A deck of cards is a physical element of the symmetric group, and
shuffling is a walk on that group. This is the classical viewpoint of
Aldous and Diaconis~\cite{aldous-diaconis}, Bayer and
Diaconis~\cite{bayer-diaconis}, Diaconis and
Shahshahani~\cite{diaconis-shahshahani}, and
Saloff-Coste~\cite{saloff-coste}: repeated shuffling is a random walk
on $\Sn$, equivalently a walk on a Cayley graph, whose mixing is
governed by the generators. Hand ciphers in the lineage of Schneier's
\emph{Solitaire}~\cite{schneier-solitaire} use the same object as a
keyed state machine rather than a source of randomness; Solitaire is
known to be statistically distinguishable from
ideal~\cite{crowley,shiu}, and modern hand ciphers such as
ElsieFour~\cite{kaminsky} are designed against such weaknesses.

This note isolates the graph-theoretic foundations of one such cipher,
the Spider cipher, independent of any cryptographic claim. Three
questions are natural for any deterministic shuffle used as a state
machine, and each is a property of the underlying Cayley graph:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Vertex transitivity.} Does the graph look the same from
every state? (Section~\ref{sec:vt}.)
\item \emph{Connectivity.} Do the moves reach every one of the $n!$
states? (Section~\ref{sec:conn}.)
\item \emph{Injectivity radius.} How long do distinct move sequences
stay distinct? (Section~\ref{sec:r}.)
\end{enumerate}

The first two have clean answers: the graph is vertex-transitive
(Proposition~\ref{prop:vt}), and the plain generators generate $\Sn$
for every even $n$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:all-n}), so the graph is connected;
the perfect variant is handled at $n=40$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:conn-perfect}). For the third we give exact
small-radius results and relate the general behavior to a counting
bound. Whether the generic behavior of such
generators is provable, rather than computational, is an instance of
open problems on diameters and mixing of $\Sn$ under arbitrary
generators~\cite{helfgott-seress-zuk,babai-seress}.

\paragraph{Computer assistance.} Several statements below are finite
computations: a specific relator holds, a bounded search finds no
collision, a count is exact, or a group order equals $40!$. These are
machine-checked in an accompanying test suite, cited as
\textsf{FACTS}($\cdot$); the proofs reduce the structural claims to
these finite checks.

\section{The Spider Graph}\label{sec:setup}

Fix an even $n$ (the deck size; $n=40$ for the cipher). Identify a
\emph{deck} with a permutation $d\in\Sn$, writing $d=[d_0,\dots,
d_{n-1}]$ for the card values in positions $0,\dots,n-1$. The identity
deck is $\id=[0,1,\dots,n-1]$.

\begin{definition}[Cut]
For $c\in\Zn$ the \emph{cut} $t^{c}\in\Sn$ acts on positions by
$(t^{c}d)_i = d_{(i+c)\bmod n}$. The cuts form a cyclic subgroup
$T_n=\{t^{0},\dots,t^{\,n-1}\}\cong\Zn$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Back-front shuffle]
The \emph{back-front} (Monge) shuffle $\beta\in\Sn$ deals the cut deck
alternately to the back and front of a new pile:
\[
(\beta d)_{\frac n2 + i} = d_{2i},\qquad
(\beta d)_{\frac n2 - 1 - i} = d_{2i+1},\qquad 0\le i<\tfrac n2 .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Spider generators]
The \emph{plain} spider generators are $g_c = \beta\circ t^{c}$ for
$c\in\Zn$. The \emph{perfect} spider generators are
$g'_c=\sigma\circ\beta\circ t^{c}$, where $\sigma=(0\ \tfrac n2-1)$
transposes positions $0$ and $\tfrac n2-1$ (positions $0$ and $19$ for
$n=40$). A \emph{step} replaces the state $d$ by $g_c\,d$ (resp.
$g'_c\,d$); the cut index $c$ is the input.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Spider graph]
The \emph{spider graph} $\Gamma$ is the Cayley graph
$\Cay(\Sn,\{g_c\}_{c\in\Zn})$: its vertices are the decks $\Sn$, and
for each $c$ there is an arc $d\to g_c\,d$. The \emph{perfect spider
graph} $\Gamma'$ uses $\{g'_c\}$ in place of $\{g_c\}$. Let
$H=\langle g_c\rangle$ and $H'=\langle g'_c\rangle$ be the groups they
generate.
\end{definition}

Note $g_0=\beta$ (since $t^{0}=\id$), and $g'_0=\sigma\beta$. A path of
length $p$ from $\id$ spells a word
$g_{c_{p-1}}\cdots g_{c_0}\in H$, so the set of states reachable from
$\id$ is exactly $H$, and the graph is connected if and only if
$H=\Sn$.

\section{Vertex Transitivity}\label{sec:vt}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:vt}
The spider graph $\Gamma$ (and likewise $\Gamma'$) is
vertex-transitive: for any decks $v,w$ there is a graph automorphism
sending $v$ to $w$. Consequently every vertex has the same local and
global structure, and all reachability, girth, and injectivity-radius
questions may be studied from the identity deck $\id$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $h\in\Sn$ let $\rho_h$ be right translation, $\rho_h(d)=d\circ h$.
It is a bijection of the vertex set. It preserves arcs: if $d\to g_c d$
is an arc then $\rho_h(d)=dh\to g_c(dh)=(g_c d)h=\rho_h(g_c d)$ is an
arc, because the generators act on the left and right translation acts
on the right. Thus $\rho_h\in\operatorname{Aut}(\Gamma)$. Taking
$h=v^{-1}w$ gives $\rho_h(v)=v(v^{-1}w)=w$. The same argument applies
to $\Gamma'$. The right-translation action is transitive on $\Sn$, so
$\Gamma$ is vertex-transitive; in particular the rooted neighborhoods
of any two vertices are isomorphic.
\end{proof}

Vertex transitivity is what lets every later argument start at $\id$,
and it has the cryptographic reading that observing motion through the
graph reveals nothing about the starting vertex; we do not pursue that
here.

\section{Connectivity}\label{sec:conn}

We show the generators generate $\Sn$, constructively, by exhibiting a
ladder of named permutations and expressing each as a generator word.
Throughout, ``reachable'' means ``lies in $H$'' (resp.\ $H'$);
reachability is closed under composition and, since $\Sn$ is finite,
under inverses ($h^{-1}=h^{\,\mathrm{ord}(h)-1}$).

\subsection{The plain graph}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:conn}
For $n=40$ the plain spider generators generate $\Sn$: $H=\Sn$. Hence
$\Gamma$ is connected on all $40!$ vertices.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We climb a ladder, each rung a reachable permutation.

\emph{(i) The shuffle and its inverse.} $\beta=g_0$ is a generator,
hence reachable, and so is $\beta^{-1}=\beta^{\,\mathrm{ord}(\beta)-1}$.

\emph{(ii) All cuts.} For each $c$, $g_c=\beta t^{c}$ is reachable, so
\[
t^{c}=\beta^{-1}g_c=\beta^{-1}(\beta t^{c})
\]
is reachable. Thus $T_n\subseteq H$.

\emph{(iii) Reversal.} Let $r=[\,n-1,n-2,\dots,0\,]$ reverse the deck.
Then
\[
r=\beta^{-1}\,t^{\,n/2}\,\beta ,
\]
a product of reachable elements (\textsf{FACTS(RReachable)}). Indeed
conjugating the half-deck rotation by the shuffle reverses order.

\emph{(iv) Pair exchange.} Let $x=(0\,1)(2\,3)\cdots(n-2\ n-1)$ swap
adjacent pairs. Then
\[
x=\beta^{-1}\,r\,\beta
\]
is reachable (\textsf{FACTS(XReachable)}).

\emph{(v) An adjacent transposition.} Let
$b_{i,i\oplus 1}$ transpose the cards in positions $i$ and
$i\oplus 1=(i+1)\bmod n$. Then
\[
b_{i,i\oplus1}
= t^{-(i\oplus1)}\,t^{\,n-1}\,x\,t\,r\,\beta^{-1}\,t^{\,21}\,\beta\,t^{\,i\oplus1},
\]
a product of elements from (i)--(iv) and $T_n$
(\textsf{FACTS(BReachable)}).

\emph{(vi) All transpositions.} For $i<j$, $b_{i,j}=t^{-i}b_{0,j-i}t^{i}$,
so it suffices to reach $b_{0,k}$. Now $b_{0,1}$ is the case $i=0$ of
(v), and for $k>1$,
\[
b_{0,k}=b_{k-1,k}\,b_{0,k-1}\,b_{k-1,k},
\]
so by induction every transposition is reachable
(\textsf{FACTS(SReachable)}).

\emph{(vii) Conclusion.} The transpositions generate $\Sn$, so
$H=\Sn$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Steps (i)--(vi) are exact group identities; the only computation is
verifying each named element equals the stated word in $\Sn$ (a
composition of fixed permutations), which the cited \textsf{FACTS}
checks. The argument is the classical one---reach the cut subgroup,
build an adjacent transposition by conjugation, then all
transpositions---specialized to these generators.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The perfect graph}

The perfect generators satisfy the same cut identity, since the extra
swap cancels:
\[
g'^{-1}_0\,g'_c=(\sigma\beta)^{-1}(\sigma\beta t^{c})
=\beta^{-1}\sigma^{-1}\sigma\beta\,t^{c}=t^{c},
\]
so $T_n\subseteq H'$ as before. What changes is the entry point: we can
no longer isolate $\beta$ (that would require $\sigma$, a transposition,
which is what we are trying to build), so a transposition must be
produced directly. It is, by a short relator.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fish}
For the perfect variant with the first-last swap
$\sigma_{0,n-1}=(0\ n-1)$ (rather than $(0\ \tfrac n2-1)$), the nine-fold
$c=2$ step is a single transposition:
\[
(\,\sigma_{0,n-1}\,\beta\,t^{2}\,)^{9}=(1\ \ n-1)\qquad(n=40).
\]
For the design variant $\sigma=(0\ \tfrac n2-1)$, the nine-fold $c=2$
step is a $7$-cycle, and the commutator of that cycle with a cut
conjugate of it is a $3$-cycle.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Finite computations (\textsf{FACTS(FirstLastFish)},
\textsf{FACTS(PerfectFish)}, \textsf{FACTS(Perfect3Cycle)}). The
mechanism is that the plain step $\beta t^{2}$ has order $9$ on the
identity deck, and the swap, commuted through the nine equal steps,
collapses on the short orbit of its support; for $(0\ n-1)$ both swap
endpoints lie on the length-$3$ orbit of $\beta t^{2}$ and the trail
collapses to one transposition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:conn-perfect}
For $n=40$ the perfect spider generators (either $\sigma=(0\,19)$ or the
first-last swap) generate $\Sn$, so $\Gamma'$ is connected on all $40!$
vertices.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fish} a transposition (resp.\ a $3$-cycle) is
reachable. Conjugating by the reachable cuts $T_n$ translates it to a
family of transpositions (resp.\ $3$-cycles); for the first-last swap,
cut conjugates of $(1\ n-1)$ give every distance-$2$ swap, conjugation
of $(0\ 2)$ through $g'_0$ gives an adjacent swap, and cuts and the
bubble identity of Theorem~\ref{thm:conn}(vi) give all transpositions
(\textsf{FACTS(FirstLastCuts,FirstLastAdjacent,FirstLastSwaps)}). For
the design swap, translated $3$-cycles generate the alternating group
$A_n$, and any odd reachable element (a single cut $t^{c}$ with $c$ odd
is an $n$-cycle, odd for even $n$) lifts $A_n$ to $\Sn$. Either way
$H'=\Sn$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Independent check]\label{prop:ss}
A Schreier--Sims stabilizer-chain computation gives
$|H|=|H'|=40!$ for the plain and both perfect variants. Since $\Sn$ is
the only subgroup of $\Sn$ of order $n!$, this certifies $H=H'=\Sn$
independently of the constructive ladder, confirming
Theorems~\ref{thm:conn} and~\ref{thm:conn-perfect}. The same
computation shows the action is primitive: no nontrivial block system
$\bmod\,d$ with $d\mid 40$ is preserved.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Connectivity for all even deck sizes}\label{sub:alln}

The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:conn} reduced the plain case to finite
checks at $n=40$. We now remove $n=40$ entirely: the plain spider
generates $\Sn$ for \emph{every} even $n$, by closed-form identities
uniform in $n$. Throughout, $h=n/2$ and $\tau=t^{1}$ is the unit cut,
$\tau(p)=(p-1)\bmod n$; recall $\beta$ acts by $\beta(2i)=h+i$,
$\beta(2i+1)=h-1-i$.

\begin{lemma}[Reduction]\label{lem:reduce}
$H=\langle g_c\rangle=\langle\beta,\tau\rangle$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\beta=g_0\in H$ and $\tau=t^{1}=\beta^{-1}g_1\in H$, so
$\langle\beta,\tau\rangle\subseteq H$; conversely each
$g_c=\beta t^{c}=\beta\tau^{c}\in\langle\beta,\tau\rangle$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Reversal]\label{lem:rev}
For every even $n$, $\ \beta^{-1}\,\tau^{\,h}\,\beta=R$, where $R(p)=n-1-p$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The inverse shuffle is
$\beta^{-1}(q)=2(q-h)$ for $q\ge h$ and $\beta^{-1}(q)=n-1-2q$ for
$q<h$. Compute $\beta^{-1}\tau^{h}\beta(p)$ with $\tau^{h}(q)=q-h$.
If $p=2i$ then $\beta(p)=h+i$, $\tau^{h}$ sends it to $i<h$, and
$\beta^{-1}(i)=n-1-2i=n-1-p$. If $p=2i+1$ then $\beta(p)=h-1-i$,
$\tau^{h}$ sends it to $h-1-i-h\equiv n-1-i\ge h$, and
$\beta^{-1}(n-1-i)=2(n-1-i-h)=2(h-1-i)=n-1-p$. In both cases the value
is $n-1-p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Pair exchange]\label{lem:x}
For every even $n$, $\ \beta^{-1}R\,\beta=x$, where
$x=(0\,1)(2\,3)\cdots(n-2\ n-1)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $R(q)=n-1-q$: if $p=2i$, $\beta(p)=h+i$, $R$ gives $h-1-i<h$, and
$\beta^{-1}(h-1-i)=n-1-2(h-1-i)=2i+1$. If $p=2i+1$, $\beta(p)=h-1-i$, $R$
gives $h+i\ge h$, and $\beta^{-1}(h+i)=2i$. So $x$ swaps $2i\leftrightarrow
2i+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Primitivity]\label{lem:prim}
For every even $n$, $H=\langle\beta,\tau\rangle$ is primitive.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$H$ is transitive ($\tau$ is an $n$-cycle), and $\langle\tau\rangle$
acts regularly, so any block system of $H$ is one of $\langle\tau\rangle$,
namely the residue partition $\mathcal P_e=\{\,\{p:p\equiv j \bmod e\}\,\}$
for a divisor $e\mid n$ with $1<e<n$ (these are the cosets of the
subgroups of $\mathbb Z_n$). It suffices that $\beta$ preserves no
$\mathcal P_e$. Consider the block $B_0=\{0,e,2e,\dots\}$ (here $e\le
h<n$, so $e\in B_0$). Then $\beta(0)=h$, while
\[
\beta(e)=\begin{cases} h+\tfrac{e}{2}, & e\text{ even},\\[2pt]
h-1-\tfrac{e-1}{2}, & e\text{ odd},\end{cases}
\qquad
\beta(e)-\beta(0)=\begin{cases}\tfrac{e}{2},\\[2pt] -\tfrac{e+1}{2}.\end{cases}
\]
Since $0<\tfrac{e}{2}<e$ and $0<\tfrac{e+1}{2}<e$ for $e\ge2$, in either
case $\beta(e)\not\equiv\beta(0)\pmod e$. Thus $\beta(B_0)$ meets two
residue classes and is not a block, so $\beta$ does not preserve
$\mathcal P_e$. Hence $H$ has no nontrivial block system.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[A transposition]\label{lem:transp}
Let $\sigma=\beta^{-1}\tau\beta$, which by direct computation acts as
$\sigma(0)=1$, $\sigma(p)=p-2$ for even $p\in[2,n-2]$, $\sigma(p)=p+2$
for odd $p\in[1,n-3]$, and $\sigma(n-1)=n-2$. Then for every even $n$,
\[
a:=\tau^{\,n-2}\,x\,\tau\,x\,\sigma\,\tau=(0\,1),
\]
a single transposition, where $x$ is as in Lemma~\ref{lem:x}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
All factors are reachable (Lemmas~\ref{lem:reduce}--\ref{lem:x}). Apply
$a$ rightmost-first, using $\tau(p)=p-1$, $\tau^{\,n-2}(p)=p+2$, the
closed form of $\sigma$, and $x(2i)=2i+1$, $x(2i+1)=2i$. For even
$p\in[2,n-2]$: $\tau\!:p\mapsto p-1$ (odd), $\sigma\!:\mapsto p+1$,
$x\!:\mapsto p$, $\tau\!:\mapsto p-1$, $x\!:\mapsto p-2$,
$\tau^{n-2}\!:\mapsto p$. For odd $p\in[3,n-1]$: $\tau\!:\mapsto p-1$
(even), $\sigma\!:\mapsto p-3$, $x\!:\mapsto p-2$, $\tau\!:\mapsto p-3$,
$x\!:\mapsto p-2$, $\tau^{n-2}\!:\mapsto p$. So $a$ fixes every
$p\notin\{0,1\}$. Finally $a(0)$: $\tau\!:\!0\mapsto n-1$,
$\sigma\!:\mapsto n-2$, $x\!:\mapsto n-1$, $\tau\!:\mapsto n-2$,
$x\!:\mapsto n-1$, $\tau^{n-2}\!:\mapsto 1$; and $a(1)$:
$\tau\!:\!1\mapsto 0$, $\sigma\!:\mapsto 1$, $x\!:\mapsto 0$,
$\tau\!:\mapsto n-1$, $x\!:\mapsto n-2$, $\tau^{n-2}\!:\mapsto 0$. Thus
$a=(0\,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:all-n}
For every even $n$ the plain spider generators generate the full
symmetric group: $\langle\beta,\tau\rangle=\Sn$. Hence the plain spider
graph is connected on all $n!$ states, for every even deck size.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:transp}, $(0\,1)\in H$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:reduce}
each cut $\tau^{i}\in H$, so the conjugates
$\tau^{-i}(0\,1)\tau^{\,i}=(i\ \ i{+}1)$ are all in $H$ for
$i=0,\dots,n-2$. The adjacent transpositions $(0\,1),\dots,(n{-}2\ n{-}1)$
generate $\Sn$, so $H=\Sn$. (Equivalently, by Lemma~\ref{lem:prim} $H$
is primitive and contains the transposition $(0\,1)$, and a primitive
group containing a transposition is $\Sn$ by Jordan's theorem.)
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:all-n} subsumes the plain case of
Theorem~\ref{thm:conn} ($n=40$) with no finite verification: the
identities of Lemmas~\ref{lem:rev}--\ref{lem:transp} hold by the
closed-form computations above for all even $n$. The Schreier--Sims
order computation (Proposition~\ref{prop:ss}) remains an independent
cross-check and, for $n=40$, also disposes of the perfect variant,
whose generation we proved separately
(Theorem~\ref{thm:conn-perfect}); a uniform-in-$n$ proof for the
perfect variant is left open (its entry transposition, the fish of
Lemma~\ref{lem:fish}, is variant- and $n$-specific).
\end{remark}

\section{Injectivity Radius (Free Depth) and Girth}\label{sec:r}

Connectivity says the moves reach everywhere; the next question is how
much information a move sequence carries before it begins to repeat
states.

\begin{definition}[Free depth]
The \emph{free depth} $r$ of the spider graph is the largest $r$ such
that the map from cut words of length $\le r$ to $\Sn$,
$(c_1,\dots,c_k)\mapsto g_{c_k}\cdots g_{c_1}$, is injective. Equivalently,
the rooted out-tree of depth $r$ is a genuine tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Girth and relators]
A \emph{relator} of length $L$ is a nonempty cut word equal to the
identity of $\Sn$ (a closed walk through any, hence every, vertex). The
\emph{girth} $\gamma$ is the least relator length. Let $c_L$ be the
number of relators of length $L$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{A counting bound}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:count}
For any out-degree-$n$ vertex-transitive graph on $n!$ vertices,
$r\le \gamma-1$ and
$\gamma\le 1+\big\lceil \log_n\!\big((n-1)\,n!+1\big)\big\rceil$. For
$n=40$ this gives $\gamma\le 31$, and a relator of length below
$30$ is not forced by counting.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A relator of length $\gamma$ makes the empty word and a length-$\gamma$
word collide, so $r\le\gamma-1$. The number of words of length $\le L$
is $(n^{L+1}-1)/(n-1)$; once this exceeds $n!=|\Sn|$ two words must
collide, giving a relator. The least $L$ with
$(n^{L+1}-1)/(n-1)>n!$ is the stated bound; for $n=40$,
$(40^{31}-1)/39>40!>(40^{30}-1)/39$.
\end{proof}

The birthday heuristic sharpens the typical scale: among the $n^{k}$
words of length $k$ mapping into $n!$ states, coincidences become
likely near $n^{2k}\approx n!$, i.e.\
\[
r^{\*}\approx \tfrac12\log_n n! \approx 15\quad(n=40).
\]
This is an estimate, not a theorem; the certified statements follow.

\subsection{Girth of the plain variant}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:girth}
The plain spider graph for $n=40$ has girth exactly $9$: no cut
sequence of length $\le 8$ is a relator, and
$(\beta t^{2})^{9}=\id$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The relator $(\beta t^{2})^{9}=\id$ is a finite computation
(\textsf{FACTS(IReachable)}), giving $\gamma\le 9$. For $\gamma\ge 9$,
note a relator of length $L\le 8$ is a closed walk $\id\to X\to\id$;
splitting it at its midpoint puts a non-identity deck $X$ in both the
forward ball $B_4$ (reachable in $\le 4$ forward steps) and the
backward ball $B_4^{-}$ (reachable in $\le 4$ inverse steps), since
$\lceil L/2\rceil\le 4$. A meet-in-the-middle computation builds both
balls (about $2.6\times10^{6}$ decks each, the full
$1+40+\cdots+40^4$ tree, so the forward map is already injective to
depth $4$) and finds their intersection is $\{\id\}$
(\textsf{FACTS(ImperfectGirth)}). Hence no relator of length $\le 8$
exists and $\gamma=9$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The plain free depth is at most $8$, and at least $4$
(the depth-$4$ ball is a tree, by the same computation). The perfect
variant has no relator of length $\le 8$ either; its single-cut first
return is at least $30$ (\textsf{FACTS(Cycles)}), at the counting bound.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Relators and the leak rate}

A single relator $w$ of length $L$ is not an isolated coincidence: for
any prefix word $s$, the word $s\cdot w$ reaches the same state as
$s$, so $w$ induces about $n^{r-L}$ collisions among words of length
$\le r$. Counting these gives a rate independent of $r$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:leak}
The fraction of length-$r$ words involved in a collision induced by
relators is, to leading order,
\[
\sum_{L\ge\gamma} \frac{c_L}{n^{L}} .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A length-$L$ relator $w$ and each of the $\sum_{m\le r-L} n^{m}\sim
n^{r-L}$ prefixes $s$ yield the colliding pair $(s,\,s\cdot w)$; summing
over the $c_L$ relators of each length and dividing by the $\sim n^{r}$
words of length $r$ gives $\sum_L c_L n^{r-L}/n^{r}=\sum_L c_L/n^{L}$.
By vertex transitivity $c_L$ is well defined independent of base point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:counts}
For $n=40$: the plain variant has $c_9=1$ (the relator
$(\beta t^{2})^{9}$ is the unique length-$9$ relator; no mixed cut
sequence returns in $9$) and $c_{10}=0$. The perfect variant
($\sigma=(0\,19)$) has $c_9=c_{10}=0$. Hence the plain relator leak rate
is $40^{-9}+O(40^{-12})$ and the perfect rate is below $40^{-10}$
(certified), with the next single-cut relator near length $30$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$c_L$ equals the number of distinct decks reachable forward in exactly
$\lceil L/2\rceil$ steps and backward in $\lfloor L/2\rfloor$ steps,
counted by intersecting balls. The counts $c_9=|B_5\cap B_4^{-}|$ and
$2c_9+c_{10}=|B_6\cap B_4^{-}|$ are obtained by storing the small
backward ball ($B_4^{-}$, strictly sorted) and enumerating the forward
ball by depth-first search, probing each deck; the searches give the
stated values (\textsf{FACTS(Relations)}). The plain $c_9=1$ identifies
$(\beta t^{2})^{9}$ as the only length-$9$ relator; $c_{10}=0$ and the
perfect zeros follow from empty intersections.
\end{proof}

\section{Status of the Results and Open Problems}

The structural results---vertex transitivity
(Proposition~\ref{prop:vt}), the constructive generation of $\Sn$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:conn},~\ref{thm:conn-perfect}), the counting bound
(Proposition~\ref{prop:count}), the relator-to-collision amplification
(Proposition~\ref{prop:leak})---are proofs. The remaining inputs are
finite computations of three kinds, each machine-checked: (a) a named
permutation equals a given generator word (the ladder rungs and the
relators of Lemma~\ref{lem:fish}); (b) a bounded ball search has empty
intersection (the girth, Theorem~\ref{thm:girth}); (c) an exact count
or a group order (Theorem~\ref{thm:counts},
Proposition~\ref{prop:ss}). The birthday scale $r^{\*}\approx15$ is a
heuristic; the certified free depth is the prefix that exhaustive
search reaches.

Three questions are open and, we believe, genuinely hard:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{General $n$, perfect variant.} Theorem~\ref{thm:all-n}
settles the plain variant for all even $n$. Does the perfect variant
(either swap) also generate $\Sn$ for all even $n$? Its entry
transposition---the fish of Lemma~\ref{lem:fish}---is variant- and
$n$-specific, so a uniform proof would need a different argument (the
cut subgroup and primitivity carry over, but isolating a transposition
does not).
\item \emph{A provable lower bound on the free depth.} A collision at
depth $m$ is a relation $uv^{-1}=e$ with $u,v$ forward words of length
$\le m$ and distinct first letters---one forward arm, one backward
arm. The all-forward case (one empty arm) is a directed cycle, giving
$r\le\gamma-1$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:count}); but for the perfect
variant the binding collision is a \emph{balanced} relation (both arms
$\approx 8$) well below the girth ($\approx 30$), so the bound is not a
girth bound and cannot be reduced to one. The only clean lower bound we
have is $r\ge 2$: $\beta$ does not normalize the cut subgroup
($\beta^{-1}T\beta\cap T=\{1\}$, since $\beta^{-1}\tau\beta$ is not a
rotation), so no depth-$2$ relation exists; each further depth is a
finite but rapidly growing coset condition. Can $r$ be bounded below by
$\Omega(\log n)$, or matched to the birthday scale $\tfrac12\log_n n!$,
by proof rather than exhaustion? A provable lower bound of this kind
would be a girth/expansion estimate for an explicit $\Sn$ Cayley graph
at near-optimal scale---open territory in general
\cite{helfgott-seress-zuk,babai-seress}. The most promising route may
be a Bayer--Diaconis/Reeds bit-accumulation argument: each inverse
back-front step resolves a card to one half (one bit), as each inverse
riffle does, suggesting injectivity could be tracked through the
accumulated bits---if the interleaved cut-rotations, which permute the
bit registers, can be controlled.
\item \emph{Deterministic vs.\ random mixing.} The classical results
\cite{aldous-diaconis,bayer-diaconis,diaconis-shahshahani} concern
\emph{random} shuffles. Does the deterministic cut-and-back-front walk
mix on the same scale, and do the spectral and strong-uniform-time
tools transfer to it? More broadly, the diameter and mixing of $\Sn$
under arbitrary fixed generators remain open
\cite{helfgott-seress-zuk,babai-seress}.
\end{enumerate}

The machinery used here is entirely standard; what appears to be new is
the object---the Cayley graph of $\Sn$ generated by a cut composed with
a Monge shuffle---together with the uniform-in-$n$ proof that it is
connected for every even deck size (Theorem~\ref{thm:all-n}) and the
explicit determination of its girth and low-order relator counts.

